CAT = "phcleCrimLaw"; SEG = "LAW"
# Criminal Law and Jurisprudence: the Revised Penal Code, Books 1 and 2, and special penal laws.
# (stem, key, [3 wrong], explanation). Key authored first; build.py rotates its slot.
Q = [
("Under the Revised Penal Code, felonies are committed by means of:",
 "Deceit or fault", ["Intent or motive", "Act or omission only", "Malice or negligence of others"],
 "Article 3 says felonies are committed by deceit (dolo, with deliberate intent) or by fault (culpa, from imprudence, negligence, lack of foresight or lack of skill). Motive is not an element of a felony."),
("An offender has performed all the acts of execution that would produce the felony, but it is not produced for causes independent of his will. The stage is:",
 "Frustrated", ["Attempted", "Consummated", "Impossible crime"],
 "Under Article 6 a felony is frustrated when all acts of execution are done but the result fails for independent causes. It is attempted when the offender has not performed all acts of execution."),
("Light felonies are punishable only when consummated, except those committed against:",
 "Persons or property", ["Public order", "National security", "Chastity"],
 "Article 7 punishes light felonies only when consummated, with the exception of light felonies against persons or property, which are punished even when attempted or frustrated."),
("Conspiracy and proposal to commit a felony are punishable:",
 "Only when the law specially provides", ["In every felony", "Only in crimes against persons", "Only when the felony is consummated"],
 "Article 8 makes conspiracy and proposal punishable only in the cases the law specifically provides, such as conspiracy to commit treason, rebellion or sedition."),
("Which is NOT a requisite of self-defense?",
 "Actual injury to the accused", ["Unlawful aggression", "Reasonable necessity of the means employed", "Lack of sufficient provocation by the defender"],
 "Article 11 requires unlawful aggression, reasonable necessity of the means used to repel it, and lack of sufficient provocation on the part of the person defending himself. The accused need not be injured first."),
("A person acts under the compulsion of an irresistible force. This circumstance is:",
 "Exempting", ["Justifying", "Mitigating", "Alternative"],
 "Irresistible force is an exempting circumstance under Article 12: there is a crime but no criminal liability because the actor lacks freedom. Civil liability falls on the person who used the force."),
("Which is an alternative circumstance under Article 15?",
 "Intoxication", ["Treachery", "Voluntary surrender", "Evident premeditation"],
 "Relationship, intoxication and degree of instruction are alternative circumstances: they aggravate or mitigate depending on the nature and effects of the crime. Treachery and evident premeditation aggravate; voluntary surrender mitigates."),
("The offender employs means that tend directly and specially to insure the execution of the crime against persons without risk to himself from any defense. This is:",
 "Treachery", ["Abuse of superior strength", "Evident premeditation", "Craft"],
 "That is the definition of treachery (alevosia) in Article 14. Abuse of superior strength looks at the disparity in force; evident premeditation looks at planning over time."),
("A person who, with knowledge of the crime, conceals the body of the victim to prevent its discovery, without having taken part as principal or accomplice, is liable as:",
 "An accessory", ["A principal by indispensable cooperation", "An accomplice", "A principal by inducement"],
 "Article 19 makes accessories those who, after the commission of the crime, conceal or destroy its body, effects or instruments to prevent discovery. Article 20 exempts certain close relatives unless they profited."),
("A person who, on a previous occasion, was convicted by final judgment of a crime embraced in the same title of the Revised Penal Code is:",
 "A recidivist", ["A habitual delinquent", "A quasi-recidivist", "A reiterant"],
 "Recidivism under Article 14 requires a prior final conviction for a crime in the same title. Habitual delinquency covers repeated convictions for specified crimes within ten years; reiteration covers prior punishment for an equal or greater crime."),
("A single act constitutes two or more grave or less grave felonies. This is a:",
 "Compound crime", ["Complex crime proper", "Continuing crime", "Special complex crime"],
 "Article 48 covers both forms. A single act producing two or more grave or less grave felonies is a compound crime; when one offense is a necessary means to commit another, it is a complex crime proper. The penalty for the most serious crime is imposed in its maximum period."),
("Which extinguishes criminal liability totally under Article 89?",
 "Absolute pardon", ["Conditional pardon", "Commutation of sentence", "Parole"],
 "Article 89 lists death of the convict, service of sentence, amnesty, absolute pardon, prescription of the crime, prescription of the penalty and, in certain crimes, marriage of the offended woman. Conditional pardon, commutation and parole only partially extinguish it."),
("The duration of reclusion perpetua, as amended by R.A. 7659, is:",
 "20 years and 1 day to 40 years", ["12 years and 1 day to 20 years", "6 years and 1 day to 12 years", "30 years and 1 day to life"],
 "R.A. 7659 fixed reclusion perpetua at 20 years and 1 day to 40 years. Reclusion temporal runs 12 years and 1 day to 20 years, and prision mayor 6 years and 1 day to 12 years."),
("A Filipino citizen who levies war against the Philippines or adheres to its enemies, giving them aid or comfort, commits:",
 "Treason", ["Rebellion", "Sedition", "Espionage"],
 "Treason under Article 114 is committed in time of war by a Filipino or resident alien who levies war against the government or adheres to its enemies. Conviction needs two witnesses to the same overt act or a confession in open court."),
("A swift attack with violence, intimidation, threat, strategy or stealth by members of the military or police against duly constituted authorities, to seize state power, is:",
 "Coup d'etat", ["Rebellion", "Sedition", "Direct assault"],
 "Article 134-A defines coup d'etat by the swift attack and the offenders' public positions. Rebellion is a public uprising and taking up of arms; sedition is a public and tumultuous uprising for lesser political ends."),
("A public officer who detains a person for a legal ground and fails to deliver him to the proper judicial authority within 36 hours, for a crime punishable by afflictive or capital penalties, commits:",
 "Delay in delivery of detained persons", ["Arbitrary detention under Article 124", "Illegal detention", "Unlawful arrest"],
 "Article 125 sets 12, 18 and 36 hours for light, less grave and afflictive or capital offenses. The arrest itself was lawful, so the crime is delay in delivery, not arbitrary detention under Article 124."),
("Killing one's legitimate spouse, or one's father, mother or child whether legitimate or illegitimate, is:",
 "Parricide", ["Murder", "Homicide", "Infanticide"],
 "Article 246 defines parricide by the relationship between offender and victim. Infanticide under Article 255 is the killing of a child less than three days old."),
("Physical injuries that incapacitate the offended party for labor for 10 days or more, but not more than 30 days, are:",
 "Less serious physical injuries", ["Serious physical injuries", "Slight physical injuries", "Mutilation"],
 "Article 265 covers incapacity or medical attendance of 10 days or more. Incapacity of more than 30 days is serious under Article 263; 1 to 9 days is slight under Article 266."),
("Taking personal property belonging to another, with intent to gain, by means of force upon things, is:",
 "Robbery", ["Theft", "Estafa", "Malicious mischief"],
 "Article 293 makes the taking robbery when done with violence or intimidation against persons or force upon things. Without either, the taking is theft under Article 308."),
("Theft committed by a domestic servant, or with grave abuse of confidence, is:",
 "Qualified theft", ["Simple theft", "Estafa with abuse of confidence", "Robbery in a dwelling"],
 "Article 310 raises the penalty by two degrees for theft by a domestic servant or with grave abuse of confidence, among other qualifying circumstances. Estafa needs juridical possession, not mere physical custody."),
("Buying or possessing an item one knows, or should know, came from robbery or theft, with intent to gain, is punished as:",
 "Fencing under P.D. 1612", ["Theft under Article 308", "Accessory to theft", "Estafa under Article 315"],
 "P.D. 1612, the Anti-Fencing Law, makes such dealing a separate crime, and possession of stolen goods is prima facie evidence of fencing. The fence need not have taken part in the theft."),
("Under R.A. 10175, a crime under the Revised Penal Code committed through information and communications technologies is punished:",
 "One degree higher", ["One degree lower", "In its maximum period only", "With the same penalty"],
 "Section 6 of the Cybercrime Prevention Act of 2012 imposes a penalty one degree higher than the Revised Penal Code or special law provides when the crime is committed through ICT."),
("Under R.A. 11053, the Anti-Hazing Act of 2018, hazing is:",
 "Prohibited in all forms", ["Allowed with written parental consent", "Allowed with school approval", "Prohibited only if injury results"],
 "R.A. 11053 amended the 1995 law, which only regulated hazing, and now prohibits all forms of hazing in fraternities, sororities and organizations. The older law allowed initiation with prior notice to school authorities."),
("Under R.A. 9262, violence against women and their children covers acts by:",
 "A current or former intimate partner", ["Any stranger to the woman", "Only her lawful husband", "Only a relative within the fourth degree"],
 "The Anti-VAWC Act of 2004 covers acts by a husband, former husband, or a person with whom the woman has or had a sexual or dating relationship or a common child. Physical, sexual, psychological and economic abuse are included."),
]
